<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhang Jinghui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhang Jinghui was a Chinese general of the Fengtian clique in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> who served the Japanese-backed state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>. He succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-xiaoxu">Zheng Xiaoxu</a> as prime minister in 1935 and held the post until the state collapsed in 1945, while Japanese officials and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> made the real decisions. Captured by Soviet forces, he died in a Chinese prison for war criminals in 1959.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-jinghui.jpg" alt="Zhang Jinghui" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhang_Jinghui2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhang Jinghui was born on 21 June 1871 in Tai'an, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a> in Liaoning Province, an area that became a battlefield in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a>. At an early age he joined the Honghuzi irregular cavalry of the Manchurian warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin">Zhang Zuolin</a>, forces that the Japanese recruited as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904 to 1905. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> died in 1916 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-army">Beiyang Army</a> split into mutually hostile factions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Zhang Jinghui deserted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin">Zhang Zuolin</a> for the Zhili clique of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-peifu">Wu Peifu</a>, but later rejoined him. He served as minister of war in the Beiyang Government from May 1926 to June 1927 and as minister of industry in the National Pacification Army Government from June 1927 to June 1928, and was then appointed governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a> and China Eastern Railway Special District in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. After Zhang Zuolin was killed in the Huanggutun Incident on 4 June 1928, his relations with the son and successor, Zhang Xueliang, deteriorated.</p><p>Following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukden-incident">Mukden Incident</a> and the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> by the Japanese Kwantung Army in 1931, Zhang called a conference on 27 September to form an emergency committee aiming at the secession of Manchuria from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He proclaimed his territory self-governing and was inaugurated as governor on 7 January 1932. He then came to terms with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and accepted the governorship of Heilongjiang Province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>. His refusal to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a> for Qiqihar caused friction with the Kwantung Army, and he was set aside when General Ma Zhanshan accepted the post on 14 February 1932. Ma revolted in April, and Zhang replaced him as minister of defence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His appointment as prime minister on 21 May 1935 was made at the instigation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> and over the objections of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a>. He is said to have spent his days copying Buddhist sutras and was nicknamed the Tofu Prime Minister in his own lifetime. He is recorded as speaking out against the Japanese administration only once, to criticise the forced sale of land to Japanese colonists. In 1943 he represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a> at the Greater East Asia Conference in Tokyo; in the same year Time magazine published a false report that he had killed his family and his Japanese adviser and then himself.</p><p>After the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> held him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> until 1950, when he was extradited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> and imprisoned at the Fushun War Criminals Management Centre. He died there of heart failure on 11 January 1959.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhang Jinghui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 16 February 1932 the Imperial Army convened Chinese governors and generals, among them <b>Zhang Jinghui</b> and Ma Zhanshan, as the Northeast Administrative Committee, which declared the Northeast provinces independent on 18 February and proclaimed Manchukuo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tai'an County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Fushun, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.38,122.42&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Tai'an County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7538 688 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zhang Jinghui"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7988 958h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7988 958h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.38,122.42&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
